  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Why Austria deserves its own China routing lens</h2>
    <p>
      Austria is the landlocked test of the "port is not the destination" rule. The country data shows
      <strong>Vienna</strong> as the capital, the currency as <strong>EUR</strong>, and a
      <strong>20% standard VAT</strong> layered on top of the <strong>EU Common Customs Tariff</strong> —
      a duty line that is uniform across every member state and varies only by HS code, typically
      <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong> with no China–EU free-trade agreement in force.
    </p>
    <p>
      The structural difference from Germany, the Netherlands or Italy is that Austria has
      <strong>no seaport of its own</strong>. Every China ocean shipment must land at someone else's port
      and then travel inland: <strong>Hamburg (DEHAM)</strong> is roughly <strong>1,000 km</strong> from
      Vienna, while the Adriatic's <strong>Koper (SIKOP)</strong> is only <strong>~480 km</strong> away —
      roughly half the inland distance. That distance gap is why Koper and Trieste quietly compete for
      Austrian cargo despite the longer sea leg down the Mediterranean: the inland haul is where the
      Austrian landed cost is actually decided.
    </p>
    <p>
      Vienna changes the equation a second way. It is both an <strong>inland Danube terminal (ATVIE)</strong>
      and a <strong>New Silk Road rail terminus</strong>, so rail can bypass the port problem entirely and
      land in roughly <strong>18–22 days</strong> instead of the ~32–43 a sea-plus-inland routing needs.
      On the commercial side, the honest position is that <strong>FCL and LCL ranges are published</strong>
      for the Hamburg corridor (MEDIUM confidence), while rail, air, express and DDP dollar figures are not —
      so those lines stay explicit request-for-quote markers instead of borrowed numbers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. Indicative freight rates from China to Austria</h2>
      <p>
        Austria inherits the published Hamburg-corridor benchmark depth from the WorldFreightHub Europe
        route data: <strong>$1,200–$4,600 for a 20ft</strong>, <strong>$1,600–$6,500 for a 40ft</strong>
        and <strong>$60–$150 per CBM for LCL</strong>, all MEDIUM confidence. Rail to Vienna is described
        in relative terms — roughly two to three times sea per container — but has no published dollar
        figure, and air, express and DDP were not published in the snapshot, so those stay LOW with a
        request-for-quote marker.
      </p>

      <DataTable
