  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. Transit times by origin port and mode</h2>
    <p>
      The China-to-Austria clock has two beats for ocean cargo: the <strong>sea leg to a transit
      gateway</strong>, then the <strong>inland leg to Vienna</strong>. The WorldFreightHub Europe route
      data puts sea transit at roughly <strong>30–38 days via the Cape of Good Hope</strong>, with the
      Red Sea reroute adding about <strong>+10–14 days</strong> versus the pre-crisis Suez routing, then
      roughly <strong>~2–5 days</strong> of inland rail/truck — a door-to-door planning window of roughly
      <strong>32–43 days</strong>. Rail from Xi'an or Chengdu to Vienna runs roughly <strong>18–22 days</strong>.
      Austrian air transit is not published, so that line stays explicit instead of borrowing a
      neighbouring airport figure.
    </p>

    <DataTable
