  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>6. Cost composition and the hidden charges that competitors miss</h2>
      <p>
        Austria's statutory stack is only the visible top layer: <strong>EU common duty on CIF</strong>
        (typically 0–12% by HS code), then <strong>20% VAT on the duty-inclusive base</strong>. Below
        that line sits the charge that is unique to a landlocked market — the
        <strong>inland gateway-to-Vienna leg</strong> — plus the operational charges the verified snapshot
        does not quantify: THC, documentation, inspection, port storage, demurrage and detention. This is
        where a cheap freight quote becomes an expensive clearance.
      </p>

      <div class="note">
        <strong>Baseline statutory landed cost = (CIF + duty) × 1.20.</strong><br />
        Duty varies by HS code, so there is no single multiplier like the Gulf's 5%. For an illustrative
        USD 10,000 CIF shipment at an assumed 5% duty: USD 500 duty gives a USD 10,500 duty-inclusive
        base, then 20% VAT of USD 2,100, giving USD 12,600 before inland and destination fees. The
        compound is <strong>1.05 × 1.20 = ×1.26</strong>, not the 25% you would get by simply adding the
        two headline rates. Rates and duty lines are subject to change — verify the current duty line and
        VAT treatment with Austrian Customs before relying on this example.
      </div>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Full cost stack</h3>
      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'item', label: 'Cost component' },
          { key: 'who', label: 'Who charges it' },
          { key: 'magnitude', label: 'Indicative magnitude' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={costRows}
        caption="The FCL/LCL ranges, EU duty and 20% VAT are MEDIUM confidence from route/customs sources. Unquantified Austrian and gateway fees are LOW confidence because the snapshot did not publish amounts."
      />
      <SourceList sources={costSources} title="Sources — landed cost & customs" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">What most guides skip</h3>
      <p>
        Five lines change the Austrian landed cost and rarely appear on a standard ocean quote:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The inland gateway-to-Vienna leg is not in the ocean quote.</strong> A Hamburg freight
          figure ends at the port; the ~1,000 km to Vienna (or ~480 km from Koper) is a separate rail/truck
          charge that must be added before the quote is usable.</li>
        <li><strong>Koper is Austria's quiet Adriatic gateway.</strong> At roughly half Hamburg's inland
          distance, Koper can win on total landed cost even when its sea leg is longer down the Med.</li>
        <li><strong>You can clear inland instead of at the port.</strong> Under EU customs transit (T1/T2,
          NCTS) cargo moves to an inland Austrian customs office in Vienna, Linz or Graz, where the 20%
          VAT is paid at the inland point.</li>
        <li><strong>VAT deferral is available.</strong> EU customs warehousing (procedures 42/63) defers
          import VAT until goods are released for free circulation — useful for inventory holders and
          re-exporters.</li>
        <li><strong>Rail regauges at the EU border.</strong> China–Europe rail runs 1435 mm standard gauge
          → 1520 mm broad gauge → back to 1435 mm at the EU border, with transshipment/regauge at points
          such as Małaszewicze (Poland) before the Poland/Germany corridor reaches Austria.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Demurrage vs detention: two clocks, two payees</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Demurrage</strong> is the charge the <strong>gateway terminal</strong> applies when import
        cargo stays beyond the allowed free time after discharge. <strong>Detention</strong> is the charge
        the <strong>ocean carrier</strong> (or rail operator) applies when the importer keeps the container
        beyond the equipment free time after collection. For Austria, the inland leg adds a second
        exposure point: a container can clear the gateway free time but still be late at the inland
        terminal, so confirm both the gateway and the inland free-time allowance in writing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Austrian and gateway free-time periods and per-day amounts are not published in the verified
        snapshot, so do not assume a Rotterdam or Antwerp schedule applies. Before booking, ask the carrier
        and the forwarder to put the gateway free time, inland free time, demurrage rate and detention rate
        in writing. The most reliable avoidance tactic is documentary readiness: register the EORI early,
        lock the TARIC code, prepare the invoice/packing list/B-L and file the Austrian declaration promptly.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Hidden charges to ask for on the quote</h3>
      <p>
        Request an itemised schedule that separates each destination line. A quote that only says "freight
        + customs" can hide <strong>THC</strong> at the gateway, a <strong>documentation fee</strong>, a
        possible <strong>customs inspection fee</strong>, <strong>port storage</strong>, the
        <strong>inland rail/truck leg</strong>, and the <strong>demurrage/detention</strong> exposure if
        release is delayed. Without those lines, a dollar-level freight quote cannot be compared or used
        for landed-cost budgeting.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>7. Compliance: Austria duty, 20% VAT, EORI &amp; conformity</h2>

    <h3>Tax and duty</h3>
    <p>
      Austria applies the <strong>EU Common Customs Tariff</strong>, so duty is identical across member
      states and depends on the HS code — typically <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong>, with no
      China–EU free-trade agreement in force. Import <strong>20% VAT</strong> (10% and 13% reduced on
      some goods) is then charged on the <strong>duty-inclusive value</strong>, so the baseline statutory
      calculation is <code>(CIF + duty) × 1.20</code>. Specific goods can still carry different duty lines
      or VAT treatment, so the HS code remains the first compliance decision.
    </p>

    <h3>EORI — register before arrival</h3>
    <p>
      The <strong>EORI number</strong> is mandatory for any import into the EU and must be registered
      <em>before</em> the goods arrive. It is the single most common first-import stumbling block for
      China-to-Austria cargo, so start the registration before the vessel or train departs, not while it
      is discharging at the gateway.
    </p>

    <h3>TARIC classification and documents</h3>
    <p>
      Austria classifies goods under the <strong>10-digit TARIC code</strong>, which drives the duty
      line, VAT treatment, permits and restricted screening. The standard document set is a commercial
      invoice, bill of lading (or rail/CMR consignment note for rail cargo), packing list and the correct
      TARIC classification, backed by your EORI number. Confirm the current declaration workflow with a
      licensed Austrian broker.
    </p>

    <h3>CE marking and REACH</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>CE marking</strong> is the EU conformity mark required for a wide range of regulated
      products — electronics, toys, machinery and medical devices, among others. <strong>REACH</strong> is
      the EU chemical regulation requiring registration for chemical substances. Both apply in Austria
      exactly as in every member state, so confirm the applicability and the required technical file
      before shipment.
    </p>

    <h3>VAT deferral and inland customs transit</h3>
    <p>
      Austria, like other EU states, allows <strong>EU customs warehousing (procedures 42/63)</strong> to
      defer import VAT until goods are released for free circulation — a working-capital lever for
      importers who hold inventory or re-export within the EU. For cargo moving from Hamburg or Koper,
      <strong>EU customs transit (T1/T2, NCTS)</strong> lets goods travel to an inland Austrian customs
      office in Vienna, Linz or Graz and clear there, paying the 20% Austrian import VAT at the inland
      point rather than at the gateway.
    </p>

    <h3>SABER/SASO is Saudi-only</h3>
    <p>
      SABER and SASO are <strong>Saudi-only</strong> conformity systems and do not apply to Austria or the
      EU. Do not copy a Saudi SABER/SASO step into an Austrian quote. Austria uses CE marking and REACH
      instead, and any product-specific approval should be confirmed with Austrian customs or your broker.
    </p>

    <h3>De-minimis and IOSS</h3>
    <p>
      The EU abolished the €22 duty-free threshold in 2021, so import VAT now applies from the first euro
      on most consignments. The <strong>€150</strong> figure is the VAT-related threshold, and the
      <strong>IOSS</strong> scheme simplifies VAT on sub-€150 consignments sold to EU buyers. Confirm the
      current thresholds and whether IOSS applies to your channel with Austrian customs before relying on
      these figures.
    </p>

    <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — Austria customs, duty, VAT & conformity" />
  </section>

